  "worked_solution": "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot \u0394x\n$$\n\nwhere $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+k\u0394x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=0.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\u0394x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{n}=\\frac{b-0}{n}\\,\u27f9\\,b=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=\\frac{k\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=2{e}^{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=2{e}^{x}$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612.$",
